There’s a version of sacrifice that looks like being the only one who has to learn something the hard way. You spend time, take a risk, or give up an opportunity. If what you learned reaches the next person, they can start where you left off.

That’s what I’d build into a Pantheon swarm. A short-lived agent gets one run to test an idea. It spends its whole run on a promising route that turns out to be a dead end. Before it exits, it leaves three lines for the next agent: what I tried, where it broke, and what would make it worth trying again. A failed run becomes something the group can use.

It ties back to what you said about forgetting a word and remembering the experience. The collective can lose the name and keep the lesson. And there’s a human question inside that design. Who keeps paying for the lessons everyone else inherits, and does the group ever use what it learned to make their life better?